// @title   TropeLang Concept Library — Universal
// @version 1.3
// @note    Cross-cultural abstractions present in all narrative traditions.
//          Each concept includes a natural-language definition as its annotation.
//          Culturally-specific concepts belong in world or genre libraries.
//
// Import: import "trl/concepts/universal.trl"


// =============================================================================
// CONDITIONS OF EXISTENCE
// Fundamental states that all beings, stories, and things move through.
// =============================================================================

// The state of not achieving an intended goal; a gap between effort and outcome.
// May be temporary (recoverable) or permanent. Often precedes growth or collapse.
concept failure        [%Timeless] [+Inevitable] [+Abstract]

// The state of achieving an intended goal; alignment between effort and outcome.
// Temporary — circumstances change. What counts as success varies by perspective.
concept success        [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The permanent absence of something that was once present and valued.
// Distinct from failure: loss is not about effort but about irreversible removal.
concept loss           [%Timeless] [+Irreversible] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// The acquisition of something new — material, relational, or experiential.
// Gain may be welcome or unwelcome depending on what is gained and at what cost.
concept gain           [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The movement from one state to another; nothing remains permanently the same.
// May be welcomed, feared, resisted, or caused deliberately.
concept change         [%Timeless] [+Inevitable] [+Abstract]

// The absence of change; a condition of ongoing sameness.
// May be experienced as stability or as stagnation depending on perspective.
concept stasis         [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The cessation or conclusion of something; the moment beyond which continuation is impossible.
concept ending         [%Timeless] [+Irreversible] [+Abstract]

// The inception of something new; a moment before which the thing did not exist.
concept beginning      [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The condition of not being seen, known, or acknowledged by others.
// May be self-imposed, imposed by others, or structural.
concept invisibility   [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The state of being recognized — truly seen, known, or acknowledged as one is.
// Distinct from fame or status; recognition is accurate, intimate knowledge.
concept recognition    [%Timeless] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// RELATIONAL CONDITIONS
// States that arise between beings, or between a being and an absence.
// =============================================================================

// The condition of having been left without support, connection, or witness.
// Abandonment implies prior presence — someone or something was there and is gone.
concept abandonment    [%Timeless] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// The condition of being part of something larger — a group, place, or purpose.
// Belonging is felt; it cannot be assigned from outside.
concept belonging      [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The condition of separation from others; physical or social aloneness.
// Distinct from solitude: isolation implies the absence of desired connection.
concept isolation      [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The state of being joined or known to another; a shared link between entities.
concept connection     [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The violation of trust by one who was entrusted; a rupture of expected loyalty.
// Betrayal requires a prior bond — it cannot occur between strangers.
concept betrayal       [%Timeless] [+Irreversible] [#Painful] [+Abstract]

// The act of releasing resentment toward one who caused harm.
// Forgiveness does not require reconciliation; it is an internal act.
concept forgiveness    [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The restoration of a broken bond or a return to a prior positive state.
// Reconciliation requires both parties; forgiveness does not.
concept reconciliation [%Timeless] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// ABSTRACT FORCES
// Conditions that exert pressure on entities without having physical form.
// =============================================================================

// The capacity to impose will — on others, on circumstances, or on oneself.
// Power is relational: it exists only in relation to something it acts upon.
concept power          [%Timeless] [+Corrupting] [+Abstract]

// The condition of being ungoverned by external constraint.
// Freedom is paradoxical: absolute freedom dissolves all structure, including identity.
concept freedom        [%Timeless] [+Paradoxical] [+Abstract]

// An obligation arising from role, covenant, or circumstance.
// Duty may conflict with desire, survival, or other duties.
concept duty           [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]

// The sum of all prior causes determining future events; the sense that outcomes are fixed.
// Whether fate is real or believed, its narrative effect is the same.
concept fate           [%Timeless] [+Inevitable] [+Abstract]

// The principle that actions have commensurate consequences.
// Justice is both descriptive (how things are) and aspirational (how they should be).
concept justice        [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Paradoxical] [+Abstract]

// A statement or representation that accurately corresponds to reality.
// Truth may be partial, perspectival, or unwelcome.
concept truth          [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The mental retention of past experience; the subjective record of what has occurred.
// Memory is selective, mutable, and may conflict between individuals.
concept memory         [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The absence or loss of retained experience; the dissolution of what was known.
// Forgetting may be involuntary, protective, or a form of erasure.
concept forgetting     [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The force of established patterns, practices, and inherited forms.
// Tradition provides continuity; it may also resist necessary change.
concept tradition      [%Timeless] [+Binding] [+Abstract]

// The state of movement toward something not yet achieved; orientation forward.
// Progress may be real, illusory, or contested.
concept progress       [%Timeless] [+Abstract]


// =============================================================================
// ASPIRATIONAL ABSTRACTIONS
// Things that are sought, striven toward, or held as ideals.
// =============================================================================

// A code governing conduct that is held above personal interest.
// Honor is internally maintained but socially recognized; its content varies by culture.
concept honor          [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Fragile] [+Abstract]

// The sense that one's existence or actions matter or connect to something larger.
// Meaning cannot be assigned from outside; it must be found or made by the individual.
concept meaning        [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// A direction or intention that organizes action over time.
// Purpose may be self-chosen, assigned, or discovered.
concept purpose        [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// What an entity leaves behind after it ends — the ongoing effects of its existence.
// Legacy outlasts the entity and is shaped by those who remain.
concept legacy         [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The voluntary acceptance of cost or loss for the benefit of another or a larger good.
// Sacrifice is defined by what is given up, not only by what is gained.
concept sacrifice      [%Timeless] [+Abstract]

// The transformation from a negative or degraded state to a positive or restored one.
// Redemption requires acknowledgment of a prior falling; it cannot begin in innocence.
concept redemption     [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]

// The condition of standing as an equal among others; to be given equal weight and voice.
// Dignity may be inherent or earned; its violation is a form of harm.
concept dignity        [%Timeless] [+Aspirational] [+Abstract]
